  (text "RP2350B + Memory subsystem (pilink-7el.3) -- RP2350B (QFN-80), 12MHz crystal,\nBOOTSEL + reset, 16MB QSPI NOR flash, 8MB QSPI PSRAM. Replaces the Pimoroni\nPico Plus 2 W's RP2350B + flash + PSRAM with the same parts, so the existing\nSDK memory layout/patches carry over. RP2350B (not the smaller RP2350A) is\nused specifically because only the B package exposes the second QSPI\ninterface this PSRAM needs -- see\n.planning/decisions/2026-11-13-esp32-bt-coprocessor-not-rm2.md for related\ncontext and pilink-7el.3's own acceptance criteria." (at 90 20 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid dfac9397-815e-45f4-981e-7ea4c5b98b66)
  )
  (text "U101 symbol/pin map: Raspberry Pi's own official MCU_RaspberryPi_RP2350:\nRP2350_80QFN symbol (81 pins incl. the GND thermal pad), taken verbatim from\nRaspberry Pi's historical RP2350B minimal-reference-design KiCad archive\n(doc RP-006442-DD-2, RP2350_80QFN_minimal.kicad_sch/.kicad_sym, KiCad 6\nformat), mirrored at github.com/arturo182/rp2350_minimal_kicad whose own\nREADME states these files were \"taken directly from Raspberry Pi's archive\n... Minimal-KiCAD.zip\" -- i.e. Raspberry Pi's own symbol, not the\nthird-party HDR/RP2350_KiCad repo the bead flags as 'untested'. Caveat\nchecked 2026-11-14: Raspberry Pi's CURRENT Minimal-KiCAD.zip\n(datasheets.raspberrypi.com/rp2350/Minimal-KiCAD.zip) has moved to a newer\nKiCad-8-native revision (RPI-RP2350A-MINIMAL_R4-S1) that only covers\nRP2350A -- the RP2350B 80-QFN minimal example has been dropped from the\ncurrent zip, so the 2024 archive above is the latest Raspberry-Pi-authored\nRP2350B symbol/footprint available, not a stale alternative to a current one.\nPin numbers/names were cross-checked against Raspberry Pi's own published\nPimoroni Pico Plus 2 (PIM724) schematic (electrokit.com mirror, 2024-08-29) --\nsame VREG_*/QSPI_*/IOVDD/DVDD pin numbers for the RP2350B used there.\nFootprint: RP2350-QFN-80-1EP_10x10_P0.4mm_EP3.4x3.4mm_ThermalVias, copied\nfrom the same official archive into hardware/footprints/RP2350_80QFN.pretty/\n(see hardware/fp-lib-table) -- this IS the Raspberry-Pi-authored footprint,\nnot the third-party-repo one the acceptance criteria warns about." (at 60.96 43 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid cf7e0e1b-c052-412a-9493-15dcf07cabd0)
  )
  (text "Crystal Y101: Abracon ABM8-272-T3, 12MHz, same part used on both\nRaspberry Pi's own RP2350B minimal reference design and Pimoroni's\nshipped Pico Plus 2. The two load caps C101/C102 appear in series as\nseen by the crystal, so CL_seen = (C1*C2)/(C1+C2) + Cstray: two 15pF\ncaps in series give 7.5pF, plus ~2-3pF of PCB/pin stray capacitance,\nlands at ~10pF -- therefore 15pF each, matching the crystal's own\ndatasheet-rated load capacitance (CL = 10pF per Abracon's ABM8 series\ndatasheet) AND Raspberry Pi's current reference design (confirmed\n2026-11-14 against datasheets.raspberrypi.com/rp2350/Minimal-KiCAD.zip,\nrevision R4-S1, July 2026 -- still 15pF, not an outdated value). Two\n10pF caps in series would instead give only 5pF (+stray ~7.5pF),\nunderloading a crystal rated for 10pF CL, which would pull its\noscillation frequency slightly off-nominal -- a small ppm-level shift\nfrom the underload, not an out-of-spec failure -- but still worth\navoiding, not a hard correctness bug. (Pimoroni's shipped board lists\nC24/C35 as 10pF with this same crystal; that's a separate design's\nchoice, not re-derived or used as the basis for this value here.)\nR101 (1k) is a series damping resistor on XOUT, present in both source\ndesigns." (at 60.96 120 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid df52e141-5816-42d1-9e92-a9874088594b)
  )
  (text "VREG (RP2350B's internal 3.3V->1.1V switched-mode core regulator) and its\nper-pin decoupling -- the half of the split documented as out-of-scope-for-\npower.kicad_sch at the end of that sheet's own notes:\n  L101 3.3uH: VREG_LX -> +1V1 (the regulated core rail).\n  C103 4.7uF: VREG_VIN (local bypass) -> GND.\n  C104 4.7uF: +1V1 (L101's output / VREG_FB node) -> GND.\n  R104 33R + C105 4.7uF: +3V3 -> VREG_AVDD RC low-pass filter -- matches the\n    exact \"RC filter of 33 ohm and 4.7uF\" called out for VREG_AVDD in\n    JLCPCB's RP2350 KiCad layout guide (jlcpcb.com/blog/how-to-design-\n    layout-with-rp2350), itself citing Raspberry Pi's RP-008280-DS\n    hardware design guide's power section (2.1 New on-chip voltage\n    regulator / 2.2 Input supply / 2.2.1 Decoupling capacitors).\n  DVDD (3 pins): 2x 100nF (C116, C117) + 1x 4.7uF bulk (C118), all\n    +1V1 -> GND -- matches the exact cap-value mix (not uniform 100nF)\n    on Raspberry Pi's own RP2350B minimal reference schematic's DVDD row.\n  IOVDD x8 + QSPI_IOVDD + USB_OTP_VDD (10 pins): 100nF each, +3V3 -> GND\n    (C106,C107,C108,C109,C110,C111,C112,C113,C114,C115) -- 10 + 2 DVDD 100nF\n    = 12 individual 100nF decouplers total, matching the bead's own ~11-12\n    estimate.\n  ADC_AVDD: 10uF (C119) to +3V3 -- separate from VREG_AVDD; this is the\n    ADC's own analog supply pin, not the regulator's.\nValues independently verified against Raspberry Pi's official\nRP-006442-DD-2 RP2350B minimal reference schematic (component-by-component\nposition/value extraction, not a visual guess), corroborated by the JLCPCB\nblog's direct quotes of the RP-008280-DS guide for the VREG_AVDD filter and\ninductor values. This corrects the task's shorthand framing of \"C1/C5=10uF\nbulk\" as part of this network: in the real reference design C1/C5 (10uF)\nare the EXTERNAL 3.3V LDO's own input/output caps (already represented as\nC1/C2 on power.kicad_sch), not part of the internal VREG_LX/AVDD network --\nthey do not belong on this sheet." (at 460 110 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 9aa93436-d414-4749-8e62-42bc52b400dd)
  )
  (text "BOOTSEL (SW101) and reset (SW102): RP2350 has no dedicated BOOTSEL pin --\npulling QSPI_SS low during the boot-ROM's boot-select check forces USB\nmass-storage bootloader mode (confirmed via JLCPCB's RP2350 layout guide,\nquoting the design guide: \"RP2350 checks the value of this I/O during the\nboot sequence, and if it is found to be a logic 0, then RP2350 reverts to\nthe BOOTSEL mode\"). SW101 + R102 (1k) pull QSPI_SS to GND when pressed,\nsame topology as Raspberry Pi's own minimal reference design (R6+SW1\nthere) and Pimoroni's shipped board (R4+SW_BOOT). No explicit pull-up on\nQSPI_SS is fitted (DNF in Raspberry Pi's own reference design too) -- the\nROM's boot check has its own internal handling and the flash's own CS\nline floats high enough between presses; RUN has an internal pull-up per\nthe RP2350 datasheet, so SW102 + R103 (1k, current limiting) is a plain\nswitch-to-GND reset, matching Raspberry Pi's reference design (R4+SW2\nthere)." (at 460 190 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 785591cd-deab-44e8-81d6-b8d1bd6a01a3)
  )
  (text "Flash U102: Winbond W25Q128JVS, 128Mbit (128Mbit / 8 bits-per-byte =\n16MB), SOIC-8, KiCad stock Memory_Flash:W25Q128JVS symbol+footprint --\nthe exact part Raspberry Pi's own RP2350B minimal reference design uses\nfor primary flash. Pimoroni's published Pico Plus 2 schematic instead\nlists \"W25Q128JVPIQ\" (Winbond's Pb-free/tray-pack SOIC-8 ordering suffix\nfor the same density/package family) -- functionally and\nfootprint-equivalent, but not the identical ordering code; exact\nJLCPCB/LCSC SKU selection is deferred to pilink-7el.8 (BOM compilation,\nwhich depends on this bead). C120 100nF is VCC-pin local decoupling.\nPSRAM U103: AP Memory APS6404L-3SQR-ZR, 8MB (64Mbit), same CS/SIO0-3/SCLK/\nVCC/GND pin MAP as the flash (verified against Pimoroni's actual shipped\nPico Plus 2 schematic, which draws both parts with an identical pin-1..8\nlayout) -- but NOT the same physical package: AP Memory's '-ZR' suffix is\nUSON-8L (~3x2mm, leadless), confirmed via LCSC's distributor listing for\nthis exact part number (C3040877), not SOIC-8. Footprint used here is\nKiCad stock Package_SON:Winbond_USON-8-1EP_3x2mm_P0.5mm_EP0.2x1.6mm as the\nclosest body-size match (3x2mm, 0.5mm pitch); AP Memory's own mechanical\ndrawing/pad geometry was not independently re-verified against it -- flag\nfor the PCB-layout stage, not resolved here. C121 2.2uF is VCC-pin local\ndecoupling (bigger than the flash's 100nF -- matches Pimoroni's real board,\nwhich also uses a bigger cap on the PSRAM's VCC than on the flash's).\nQSPI bus (SD0-3, SCLK) is shared between flash and PSRAM; each has its own\nchip-select -- QSPI_SS (dedicated pin 75) for the primary flash, GPIO47\n(ADC7-capable, repurposed as the QMI's second chip-select) for the PSRAM,\nsame GPIO Pimoroni's Pico Plus 2 W uses for this role (one of the four\nvalid secondary-CS options per Raspberry Pi's design guide: GP0/GP8/GP19/\nGP47). R105 100k pulls PSRAM_CS high since GPIOs default to pull-down at\npower-up and would otherwise assert the PSRAM's CS during boot.\nERC note: the embedded Memory_Flash:W25Q128JVS cache previously carried a\nstray pin_numbers/pin_names display override (hide + 1.016mm offset) and\nan edited Description string, neither present on the stock symbol -- an\naccidental carryover from applying this project's pin-number-hidden\nconvention to every placed symbol, including this stock-library one.\nSynced back to match the real stock Memory_Flash:W25Q128JVS symbol\nexactly (pins/graphics were already identical); kicad-cli sch erc no\nlonger reports 'Symbol has been modified in library'." (at 340 20 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 6c7ec36c-cb5f-411a-ae98-155f5b08008e)
  )
  (text "All RP2350B GPIOs not consumed by this sheet's own circuitry (display/\ninput/ESP32-UART/USB-C pins) are brought out as global labels matching\ntheir pin name (GPIOn, USB_DP/DM, SWD/SWCLK) so the other, still-empty\nchild sheets (pilink-7el.4/.5/.6/.7) can pick them up later without needing\nhierarchical sheet pins -- same cross-sheet convention already used for\n+3V3/GND between this sheet and power.kicad_sch. GPIO47 is withheld from\nthis pool (committed to PSRAM_CS, see above), and so are the 8 GPIOs\nbelow that now wire the ESP32 BT coprocessor (pilink-kt4, fixing a defect\nwhere esp32_bt.kicad_sch's ESP32_* global labels had no RP2350-side\ncounterpart at all): their GPIOn global_label was replaced in place by\nthe matching ESP32_* label (same wire/position, name only), so they are\nNOT exposed as free GPIOn labels either.\nESP32 GPIO assignment, chosen from RP2350 datasheet (datasheets.\nraspberrypi.com/rp2350/rp2350-datasheet.pdf) Table 677 \"GPIO Bank 0\nFunctions\" -- RP2350B has exactly 2 UART peripherals (UART0, UART1) per\nthat table's F2 column (F1 is SPI, not UART):\n- ESP32_HCI_RXD/TXD/RTS/CTS: UART1, full 4-wire hardware flow control,\n  all F2 on this contiguous group -- GPIO4=UART1 TX, GPIO5=UART1 RX,\n  GPIO6=UART1 CTS, GPIO7=UART1 RTS. RTS/CTS cross for modem-style flow\n  control: ESP32's RTS (output) lands on RP2350's CTS input (GPIO6);\n  ESP32's CTS (input) is driven by RP2350's RTS output (GPIO7).\n- ESP32_FLASH_RXD0/TXD0: UART0, TX/RX only (no flow control needed for\n  esptool bridge-flashing), both F2 -- GPIO0=UART0 TX drives ESP32's\n  RXD0, GPIO1=UART0 RX receives ESP32's TXD0.\n- ESP32_EN/BOOT0: plain bit-banged GPIO outputs, no peripheral function\n  needed -- GPIO20, GPIO21 (freed back into this pool by\n  pilink-3rm's d-pad-to-encoder swap, confirmed unused by any other\n  sheet via a fresh grep before picking them).\nAll 8 were confirmed free (appeared only as a standalone GPIOn global\nlabel on this sheet, with no matching pad anywhere else) by grepping\nevery populated sheet's global_label/label entries, not just the\ngeneric-pool prose claim in the bug report.\nFirmware note: RP2350 GPIOs reset to a weak internal pull-down (datasheet\ndefault), while ESP32-WROOM-32UE's own EN and IO0 pins carry onboard 10k\npull-ups to 3V3 -- those pull-ups dominate the RP2350's weak pull-down, so\nESP32_EN/BOOT0 sit high (run mode) and the ESP32 boots normally before\nfirmware ever drives GPIO20/21 itself.\n+3V3/GND are NOT re-flagged with a PWR_FLAG on this sheet (unlike\npower.kicad_sch's own +5V input flag): this sheet doesn't generate\neither rail, power.kicad_sch (pilink-7el.2) does, via U1's VO\npin and its own GND flag. Running kicad-cli sch erc on THIS file alone\ntherefore reports 2 power_pin_not_driven errors for +3V3/GND -- expected,\nnot a bug: a single child sheet checked in isolation has no visibility\ninto power.kicad_sch's regulator. ERC on the root sheet (which flattens\nthe whole hierarchy and does see it) reports 0 errors for both nets --\nsee the PR description for both ERC runs. +1V1 and VREG_AVDD, by\ncontrast, ARE flagged here (bottom-left of sheet): nothing else in the\nproject ever drives those two nets, at any level, so the flags are\ncorrect both standalone and at the root." (at 245 20 0)
    (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
    (uuid 1b2cd564-ea2a-4ba5-b7de-18daf83b71d8)
  )
)
